---
import Base from '../../../layouts/Base.astro';
import Mermaid from '../../../components/Mermaid.astro';
import { hunterbttParts, hunterbttPartHref } from '../../../data/series';
---

<Base title="Project Tap Timer — Rebuilding the Hunter BTT BLE Bridge">
	<p><a href="/">← back to home</a></p>

	<h1>Project Tap Timer — Rebuilding the Hunter BTT BLE Bridge</h1>
	<div style="margin: 0.75rem 0 1.5rem">
		<span class="badge live">● live in production</span>
		<span class="badge">rust</span>
		<span class="badge">ble</span>
		<span class="badge">mqtt</span>
		<span class="badge">reverse engineering</span>
		<span class="badge">raspberry pi zero w</span>
		<span class="badge">home automation</span>
	</div>

	<p class="tagline">
		Three battery-powered Hunter BTT-200 tap timers — two irrigation zones each, a ~10 m
		Bluetooth leash, and a vendor app the internet has not been kind to. The original
		bridge could talk to them, but slowly enough that Google Home would give up and report
		the zones offline. This is the story of how I reverse-engineered the protocol, rebuilt
		the bridge around a persistent connection, and spent a very long day at war with
		firmware that will not sit still.
	</p>

	<h2><span class="anchor-hash">#</span>the final architecture</h2>
	<Mermaid>{`flowchart LR
		HA["home automation<br/>(MQTT valves)"] <-->|"irrigation/s2c<br/>irrigation/c2s"| BR

		subgraph BR["rshunterbtt — Rust, one per timer"]
			direction TB
			CONN["persistent connection<br/>+ auth + resubscribe"]
			NOTIF["ff82 notifications<br/>state cache"]
			GUARD["interval guard<br/>(HCI monitor)"]
			CONN --> NOTIF
			CONN --> GUARD
		end

		BR <-->|"BLE GATT<br/>persistent link"| TIMER["Hunter BTT tap timer<br/>2×AA, two zones"]
		BR -->|"battery telemetry"| DB[("InfluxDB")]`}</Mermaid>

	<p>
		Each bridge owns exactly one timer. It holds a single BLE connection open, authenticates,
		subscribes to the one characteristic that pushes zone state, and translates between that
		world and plain MQTT topics. Battery telemetry goes straight to InfluxDB; zone commands
		and state come from the home automation layer.
	</p>
	<p>
		The hardware's Bluetooth range is about ten metres — plenty for a phone standing at the
		tap, useless as an always-on integration. A small board parked within range, holding the
		connection permanently, is the only shape that works. That constraint is why the bridge
		exists at all, and why "how fast can it answer a query" turned out to be the whole
		problem.
	</p>
	<p>
		None of that is remarkable. What made it interesting is that almost every assumption I
		made along the way turned out to be wrong — about where the state lived, what the status
		bytes meant, and who actually controls the connection interval.
	</p>

	<h2><span class="anchor-hash">#</span>the series</h2>
	<ul class="series-list">
		{hunterbttParts.map((p) => (
			<li>
				<span class="series-n">part {p.n}</span>
				<a href={hunterbttPartHref(p)}>{p.title}</a>
				<span class="series-blurb">{p.blurb}</span>
			</li>
		))}
	</ul>

	<h2><span class="anchor-hash">#</span>repositories</h2>
	<ul>
		<li>
			<a href="https://github.com/retsimx/rshunterbtt">retsimx/rshunterbtt</a> — the Rust
			MQTT↔BLE bridge: connection manager, notification-driven status, HCI-level interval
			control, and the resilience ladder
		</li>
	</ul>

	<h2><span class="anchor-hash">#</span>standing on the shoulders of</h2>
	<ul>
		<li>
			The OEM Android app — a Cordova app wrapping a tidy little BLE library
			(<code>com.huiyuan.ble</code>) whose decompiled protocol classes are the only real
			documentation this hardware has ever had
		</li>
		<li>
			<a href="https://www.bluez.org/">BlueZ</a> and <code>btmon</code> — the HCI event
			stream is the ground truth for everything the controller actually agreed to do
		</li>
		<li>
			<a href="https://github.com/deviceplug/btleplug">btleplug</a> — the Rust BLE layer,
			including the escape hatches that make raw-HCI work possible
		</li>
	</ul>

	<h2><span class="anchor-hash">#</span>status</h2>
	<div class="status-line">
		<span><span class="dot"></span>bridges: live on all three timers</span>
		<span><span class="dot"></span>state: notification-driven, enum-correct</span>
		<span><span class="dot"></span>interval: held by the guard at ~1 s</span>
		<span><span class="dot amber"></span>battery: under observation</span>
	</div>
</Base>
